    <briefdescription>
<para>Functions for comparing actual and expected values in tests. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>A check written as a plain boolean, such as <computeroutput>t.expect (x == 42)</computeroutput>, reports only its location when it fails. The function comparators compare two values and, when the check fails, also report both values:</para>
<para><table rows="8" cols="2"><row>
<entry thead="yes"><para>Comparator  </para>
</entry><entry thead="yes"><para>True when  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::eq (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a == b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::ne (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a != b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::lt (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a &lt; b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::le (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a &lt;= b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::gt (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a &gt; b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::ge (a, b)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a &gt;= b</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
<row>
<entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>mt::approx (a, b, e)</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry><entry thead="no"><para><computeroutput>a</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>b</computeroutput> differ by at most <computeroutput>e</computeroutput>  </para>
</entry></row>
</table>
</para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Example</title><para></para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>various<sp/>conditions&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>42))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>42&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::ne<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>43))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>not<sp/>43&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::lt<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>43))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>&lt;<sp/>43&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::le<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>43))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>&lt;=<sp/>43&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::gt<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>41))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>&gt;<sp/>41&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::ge<sp/>(compute_answer<sp/>(),<sp/>42))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;answer<sp/>is<sp/>&gt;=<sp/>42&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para>When <computeroutput>compute_answer()</computeroutput> returns 41, the human-readable reporter displays the failed checks with the compared values:</para>
<para><programlisting filename=".console"><codeline><highlight class="normal">•<sp/>Check<sp/>various<sp/>conditions</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>✗<sp/>answer<sp/>is<sp/>42<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(main.cpp:21,<sp/>41<sp/>==<sp/>42)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>✗<sp/>answer<sp/>is<sp/>&gt;<sp/>41<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(main.cpp:25,<sp/>41<sp/>&gt;<sp/>41)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>✗<sp/>answer<sp/>is<sp/>&gt;=<sp/>42<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(main.cpp:26,<sp/>41<sp/>&gt;=<sp/>42)</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">✗<sp/>Check<sp/>various<sp/>conditions<sp/>-<sp/>FAILED<sp/>(3<sp/>checks<sp/>passed,<sp/>3<sp/>failed)</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>How the values are compared</title><para><itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The operands must be comparable with the operator of the comparator; for example, <computeroutput>mt::eq (a, b)</computeroutput> does not compile when <computeroutput>a == b</computeroutput> would not.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Integers of different signedness are compared by value, as by <computeroutput>std::cmp_equal()</computeroutput>: <computeroutput>mt::eq (-1, static_cast&lt;unsigned&gt; (-1))</computeroutput> fails, although <computeroutput>-1 == static_cast&lt;unsigned&gt; (-1)</computeroutput> is true.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>C strings (<computeroutput>char</computeroutput> arrays, including the string literals, and <computeroutput>char</computeroutput> pointers) are compared by content, also with <computeroutput>std::string</computeroutput> and <computeroutput>std::string_view</computeroutput>, so <computeroutput>mt::eq (actual, "abc")</computeroutput> checks the characters, not the addresses. A null <computeroutput>char</computeroutput> pointer is equal only to another null pointer (or <computeroutput>nullptr</computeroutput>), not to an empty string.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Other arrays (including <computeroutput>uint8_t</computeroutput> buffers) are compared element by element, also with a container.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Other pointers are compared by address.</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>Values of the application types are displayed only if the application provides an <computeroutput>operator&lt;&lt;</computeroutput> for them; see <ref refid="group__micro-os-plus-micro-test-plus-cpp-api-customisation" kindref="compound">Customisation</ref>.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="par"><title>Comparing approximately</title><para>The comparison of floating-point values with <computeroutput>eq()</computeroutput> is exact, so <computeroutput>mt::eq (0.1 + 0.2, 0.3)</computeroutput> fails. To accept a difference, use <computeroutput>mt::approx (a, b, epsilon)</computeroutput>, which is true when the operands differ by at most <computeroutput>epsilon</computeroutput>, the limit included:<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>the operands and the tolerance are integral or floating-point numbers, of any type; the distance of integers is computed exactly, also for operands of different signedness;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>a negative tolerance fails all the comparisons;</para>
</listitem><listitem><para>a failed check shows the operands and the tolerance, as in <computeroutput>1.0 ~ (1.5 +/- 0.1)</computeroutput>.</para>
</listitem></itemizedlist>
</para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>approximate<sp/>values&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::approx<sp/>(read_adc<sp/>(),<sp/>512,<sp/>3))<sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;ADC<sp/>reading&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::approx<sp/>(compute_float<sp/>(),<sp/>42.0f,<sp/>0.001f))</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/><sp/>&lt;&lt;<sp/></highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;result<sp/>is<sp/>about<sp/>42&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">;</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal">});</highlight></codeline>
</programlisting></para>
<para><simplesect kind="par"><title>Comparing containers</title><para>Containers are compared with their own operators, so they must be comparable, usually by being of the same type. When the check fails, the elements of both containers are shown, up to the first 16; when two longer containers differ after them, the report adds the index of the first difference.</para>
</simplesect>
<programlisting filename=".cpp"><codeline><highlight class="normal">ts.test<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="stringliteral">&quot;Check<sp/>containers&quot;</highlight><highlight class="normal">,<sp/>[]<sp/>(</highlight><highlight class="keyword">auto</highlight><highlight class="normal">&amp;<sp/>t)<sp/>{</highlight></codeline>
<codeline><highlight class="normal"><sp/><sp/>t.expect<sp/>(mt::eq<sp/>(std::vector&lt;int&gt;{<sp/>1,<sp/>2<sp/>},<sp/>std::vector&lt;int&gt;{<sp/>1,<sp/>2<sp/>}))</highlight></codeline>
